Example: the acquisition cost is the contribution; later prices set the value.
One FUND unit is bought for 100 USD on 2023-01-01, recorded as {100 USD}.
Its closing price is 100 USD then, 110 USD on 2023-07-01, and 120 USD on
2024-01-01. Salary funds the purchase, but only FUND is selected: the salary
and cash payment are outside the report, while the fund cost contributes 100.
Opening value is 0, net contributions are 100, and ending value is 120 USD.
Profit/loss is 120 - 0 - 100 = 20 USD. The two growth factors are 110 / 100
and 120 / 110, giving a time-weighted return of 20%. The 100 USD invested for
exactly 365 days grows to 120 USD, so the money-weighted returns are also 20%.

========== CLI ==========

ledlight investment-performance --accounts 'Assets:Fund' --commodities FUND --from 2023-01-01 --to 2024-01-01 --format text

========== FILE ==========

commodity USD
  default
  format 1,000.00 USD
commodity FUND
  format 1,000 FUND
account Assets:Fund
account Assets:Cash
account Income:Salary
P 2023-01-01 FUND 100 USD
P 2023-07-01 FUND 110 USD
P 2024-01-01 FUND 120 USD

2023-01-01 Salary
  Assets:Cash  100 USD
  Income:Salary  -100 USD

2023-01-01 Purchase
  Assets:Fund  1 FUND {100 USD}
  Assets:Cash  -100 USD

========== OUTPUT ==========

Investment performance from 2023-01-01 to 2024-01-01
Instruments: 1
Opening value: 0.00 USD
Net contributions: 100.00 USD
Ending value: 120.00 USD
Profit/loss: 20.00 USD
Time-weighted return (total): 20.00 %
Time-weighted return (annualized): 20.00 %
Money-weighted return (total): 20.00 %
Money-weighted return (annualized): 20.00 %

========== WARNINGS ==========
